    "content": {
      "component": "blog_article",
      "sharepoint_id": "91",
      "title": "People Who Read the Clouds: The Tell of Tanzania's Maasai",
      "description": "",
      "date": "Sep 29, 2025",
      "read_time": "4 min read",
      "author": "Banafsha and Didar",
      "isGlobal": true,
      "banner_image": {
        "fieldtype": "asset",
        "_local": "91-Banner.jpg"
      },
      "thumbnail": {
        "fieldtype": "asset"
      },
      "languages": [],
      "show_share": true,
      "show_similar": true,
      "similar_show_header": true,
      "category": "",
      "isFeatured": null,
      "body": [
        {
          "component": "rich_text",
          "content": {
            "type": "doc",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "It has been a month since we returned home, yet the memories of Tanzania remain as vivid as the wide skies above the savannah. That place was more than a destination; it gave us room to learn, to reflect, and most importantly, to draw inspiration. And when I think back, one image always rises above the rest: the \"people who read the clouds.\""
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In today's world of accelerating climate change, people respond differently. Some seek safety in distant cities, leaving behind vulnerable homelands. Even those who work for their communities often do so from afar, keeping themselves away from the everyday hardships of rural life. It is rare to meet someone who not only returns but chooses to live fully in the very place others escape from."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "During our internship at AKU's Arusha Climate and Environmental Research Centre (AKU-ACER), we had the privilege of meeting such a person: Alais Morindat, a Maasai pastoralist, community leader, and visionary. He describes his people as \"those who read the cloud,\" a reference to their ancestral gift of interpreting skies and rainfall to make vital decisions. Meeting him in Monduli, a Maasai village about a50-minute drive from AKU-ACER, among the open grasslands, reshaped our idea of what it means to serve one's people."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The Maasai, semi-nomadic pastoralists of East Africa, have for centuries lived in harmony with their cattle, the land, and the savannah. Cattle are their lifeblood: wealth, food, culture, and spirituality. Yet climate change has unsettled this delicate rhythm. Erratic rains, longer droughts, and shrinking pastures have put livestock and livelihoods at risk. Many young people migrate to cities, searching for different futures. Alais, however, walked a different path."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "After studying abroad in Ireland and being exposed to modern comforts, he could have stayed. But he returned home. He built a modern house, yes, but he never abandoned his pastoralist identity. Instead, he created a life that blends the two worlds: modern innovations and Maasai traditions, side by side."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "One of Alais's most striking contributions has been in livestock feeding. His land sustains large herds of cattle, goats, and sheep that depend on consistent forage even as the climate grows harsher. To address this, he introduced resilient grasses, with support from AKU-ACER, including Bana grass, a high-nutrient variety that stays green year-round and requires planting only once. This ensures that even during long dry seasons, animals have enough to feed on. He also practices controlled grazing, protecting young grass until it matures, allowing the land to recover and hold water longer."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "But Alais did not stop there. On his farm, he has established sustainable plantations growing a variety of grass and fodder trees that enrich the soil while providing food for livestock. These plantations are carefully managed so that the land is not depleted but continuously renewed. Walking through his homestead, one sees a landscape that reflects both careful planning and deep respect for the earth."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Perhaps what is most inspiring is how seamlessly Alais blends modern life with pastoralist traditions. His house may have the comforts of the present day, but right outside, cattle still graze, children learn the ways of herding, and elders gather to share stories. He embodies the idea that modernity does not have to erase tradition; rather, the two can strengthen each other. He is a great example that the modern scientific world and traditional cultural life can go hand in hand."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Through indigenous wisdom and practical innovation, Alais is tackling soil erosion, a problem many experts consider unsolvable in drylands. Using local resources, simple barriers, and Maasai techniques, he has slowed runoff, regenerated degraded soils, and brought life back to barren patches. Today, his land is dotted with lush green, where only dust and erosion scars remain."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Alais is more than a climate leader. He is a storyteller, a bridge between past and present, and a living example of what it means to return and give back. His life teaches us that progress is not about abandoning roots, but about carrying them forward, enriching them with new knowledge and passing them on to future generations."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the vast savannah of Tanzania, we discovered that resilience is not written only in textbooks or policies, but in the clouds, the land, and the lives of people like Alais Morindat, those who choose to stay, rebuild, and inspire hope."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "hard_break"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
